      lines: [
        { code: '    void Update()', what: 'Every frame, like your movement and your enemy logic. A number can change at any moment — a coin is collected in a trigger callback, damage arrives from a fireball you never see coming — so a readout that is only correct sometimes is worse than no readout at all.' , why: '**The alternative is "only rewrite the text when a value changes", and here it costs more than it saves.** You would keep the last three values as fields and compare them every frame — three extra fields, and three extra chances for one of them to go stale and freeze the label forever. Reading a value is a field lookup; the expensive part is building the string, which is exactly what you would be avoiding. At this scale the honest engineering answer is the simple one, and the moment it stops being true the fix is right here: remember the last values, and rebuild the string only when one of them moves.' },
        { code: '    {', what: '' },
        { code: '        if (StatManager.original == null)', what: 'The guard — and here it genuinely earns its place. This script can run in a scene with no manager at all: the Overworld and the Platformer both contain a Player, and neither contains a StatManager. Press Play from either one and this method runs every frame with nothing to read.' , why: '**Without this line the Console fills with a NullReferenceException sixty times a second.** That does not crash the game; it buries every other message you might need, which is worse. With the line, the label simply keeps whatever it last said. Neither is a *fix* — the real fix is that the game begins in `Start (index 0)` — but the guard turns a flood into a blank label, which is a failure you can see.' },
        { code: '        {', what: '' },
        { code: '            return;', what: 'Nothing to show, so show nothing. `return` leaves the method immediately — the same early-exit shape as the death rule in your 2.7 health method.' },
        { code: '        }', what: '' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '        string statsLine = "Health: " + StatManager.original.health', what: 'Build the line. The left-hand side is a **local** — a string that exists for this frame only, which is correct: it is thrown away and rebuilt next frame anyway, and a field would just be a place for a stale one to hide.' , why: '**Every value comes from `StatManager.original`, not from the player.** The manager is the only object that still has the numbers, and the only one that survived the scene change — which is why the HUD can be trusted at all. Read them off the player and you get a fresh copy of the player\'s starting values in every new scene, which is the bug this lesson is about.' },
        { code: '                         + " / " + StatManager.original.maxHealth;', what: 'And the ceiling, so the label reads `2 / 3`. Showing the maximum and not just the current health is a small design decision worth naming: a player can only judge "am I in trouble?" against what full looks like.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '        statsLine = statsLine + "   Coins: " + StatManager.original.coins;', what: 'Appending to the string you already built. Note the shape: assign back to the same name, because a string cannot be changed in place — this makes a new one and points the name at it.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '        statsText.text = statsLine;', what: 'The write. `statsText` is the component you wired in the Inspector; `.text` is the property holding the characters on screen. Assign to it and the label changes on this frame.' , why: '**Nothing reads the text back, and nothing stores it.** The label is a *view* of the numbers, not a copy of them — it holds no game state of its own. That is what makes it impossible for the HUD and the truth to disagree. The moment you cache a number in this script "to save time", you have built a second source of truth, and it will be the wrong one.' },
        { code: '    }', what: '' },
