        <tr>
            <td><code>void Awake()</code></td>
            <td>Unity's earliest hook: every <code>Awake()</code> in a scene finishes before the
                first <code>Start()</code> runs.</td>
            <td>Order between two objects' <code>Awake()</code> methods is still not promised.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>DontDestroyOnLoad(gameObject)</code></td>
            <td>The object moves into a scene of its own that never unloads, so it survives every
                load from then on.</td>
            <td>It takes a whole <strong>GameObject</strong>, not a component.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Destroy(gameObject)</code></td>
            <td>Removes an object — how the newcomer copy takes itself out of the game.</td>
            <td>The removal is deferred to the end of the frame: a line running later in that same
                frame can still reach it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>SceneManager.LoadScene(int buildIndex)</code></td>
            <td>What <code>SceneSwitch.cs</code> calls at every cave mouth — the line that would
                destroy your stats if nothing were persistent.</td>
            <td>Hand it an <code>int</code> and it loads whatever scene sits at that
                <em>position</em> in the build list.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification — read the owner before you add a face.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>The owner.</strong> Which field is the only reason your numbers survive a
                load, and what exactly does <code>static</code> change about where it lives? Which
                hook claims it, and what does the other branch do with a second copy?</li>
            <li><strong>The seam.</strong> Which methods may write health and coins, and where does
                the ceiling live — at the call site, or inside the method that owns the number?</li>
            <li><strong>The screen.</strong> Where does the HUD live, and what would one that read
                from the player show after a scene change?</li>
            <li><strong>The view.</strong> What state does <code>HudController</code> own? If the
                answer is none, say what that buys you the first time a number changes.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Three ways to make a number survive</h3>
        <p>All three are real Unity techniques. Compare what each one costs you:</p>
        <table>
            <tr><th>Approach</th><th>How it works</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>A <code>static</code> field on its own</strong></td>
                <td>Belongs to the <em>class</em> — destroying the objects does not touch it.</td>
                <td>No lifecycle: you cannot reset it cleanly, and in the editor it survives between Play sessions, so testing lies to you.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>A persistent singleton object</strong> — <em>this project</em></td>
                <td>One object registers as <em>the</em> instance and asks Unity not to destroy it.</td>
                <td>You must handle the <em>second</em> copy a scene load brings.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>A ScriptableObject asset</strong></td>
                <td>Lives in a project asset rather than in a scene, so no scene load can touch it.</td>
                <td>A new concept and one more asset to manage — and it survives Play sessions too, which is either the point or a nuisance.</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
        <ol>
<li>Your stats should reset when you start a new game, and survive a scene change. Which of the three can express both of those, and which one cannot express the first?</li>
<li>Start (index 0) already contains its own StatManager object. If you walk back to it later, what will be there — and what happens if nothing stops it from registering itself a second time?</li>
<li>Why can position <em>not</em> be handled this way? What is different about position that makes <code>SceneSwitch.cs</code> handle it instead?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderWalkthrough(WALK_VERIFY)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: The HUD, and Where the Game Begins</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        One decision is left, and it is yours: <strong>which scene should the game actually begin
        in?</strong> A Canvas is <em>not</em> persistent — it exists only in the scene that contains
        it.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Approach</th><th>What you do</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Move the Overworld's Canvas into <code>Start (index 0)</code></strong></td>
            <td>Drag the Canvas and its <code>Text (TMP)</code> child across, then repair whatever
                breaks.</td>
            <td>Less setup, but a Canvas carries its scene's wiring with it: any button or listener
                that pointed at an Overworld object comes across as <em>Missing</em>, and the thing
                it used to do quietly stops happening.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Leave it alone and build a fresh Canvas in <code>Start (index 0)</code></strong></td>
            <td>One menu item: <code>GameObject ▸ UI ▸ Text - TextMeshPro</code>.</td>
            <td>A second Canvas exists in the project, and the Overworld's still switches off when
                you leave it — harmlessly, because nothing is drawn on it. You break nothing you did
                not write.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        Either is defensible. This lesson builds a fresh one, because that path leaves the wiring you
        inherited exactly as you found it. Whichever you choose, <strong>write down your reason
        before you start</strong>: the checkpoint asks you for it.
    </p>
</div>

